# 2026-10-10 decor comparison: vs Matterport
EXTRA_POSTS.append({'slug': 'arqr360-vs-matterport-decor-stores', 'title': 'ARQR360 vs Matterport: What a Decor Store Actually Gets from Each', 'date': 'October 10, 2026', 'description': 'Comparing Matterport and ARQR360 for your decor store? Matterport captures your showroom as a walkable 3D tour. ARQR360 puts each product at true size in the customer’s room. An honest look at price, setup, and which one sells your products.', 'body': '<figure class="postimg"><img src="/blog/arqr360-vs-matterport-decor-stores/images/hero.jpg" alt="Shopper previewing a ceramic floor vase at true size in her living room through her phone" loading="lazy"><figcaption>She sees the true size in her own room before she buys.</figcaption></figure><p>Picture a decor store owner in Houston. She sells ceramic vases, wall mirrors, and table lamps online. A friend shows her a Matterport tour of an apartment listing and says, you should do this for your shop. The tour looks stunning. She wonders if it will sell her products.</p><p>It might, and it might not. Matterport and ARQR360 solve different problems for a decor store. This is the honest version of which one does what, what each costs, and when each one is worth your money.</p><h2>What Matterport is</h2><p>Matterport is the company behind the digital twins you see in real estate listings. It turns a real physical space into a walkable 3D tour on your website. Visitors click through your shop, look around, and zoom in on the shelves. It started in property, and retailers now use it for virtual showroom tours.</p><p>The key fact: Matterport captures your space. The customer visits your shop from their couch. Your products never leave your walls.</p><h2>What ARQR360 is</h2><p>ARQR360 is a done-for-you AR catalogue for independent decor retailers. You send photos of your bestselling pieces and your real listed dimensions. We build true-to-size AR (Augmented Reality) views from them and hand you links and QR codes that open each view in the customer\'s phone browser. No app, no platform to learn, no 3D team.</p><p>The key fact: ARQR360 puts each product in the customer\'s room. The customer checks whether your mirror fits their wall, not whether your shop looks nice.</p><h2>Pricing: per-space subscription vs one flat rate</h2><p>Matterport publishes its plans. Current listings put the Starter plan around $10 a month for a few Active Spaces, and Pro and Business from $69 a month, with Enterprise on quote. The subscription covers the hosting of your captured spaces. The capture itself is extra: you scan with a phone or a 360 camera, or you pay for their Capture Services in hundreds of cities. Their pro LiDAR camera costs thousands of dollars. So the real first-year cost is the subscription plus whatever you spend getting the space captured well.</p><p>ARQR360 publishes its price: $199 one-time setup and $19 a month, locked for life for the first 50 stores. Every AR (Augmented Reality) view is built and hosted as part of it. No product cap, no per-view fee, no capture hardware to buy. You already have the photos.</p><h2>Setup effort: scanning your space vs sending photos</h2><p>A Matterport project means capturing your showroom. You clear the space, scan every room, process the model, and keep it updated as your displays change. A rescan is a real job, so shops tend to do it once and watch it go stale as stock rotates. That is fine for a flagship that rarely changes. It is work for a store that refreshes displays every season.</p><p>With ARQR360 the project is: send photos of your bestselling vases, mirrors, and lamps, bestsellers first. About seven days later your AR views are live, as links on your product pages and QR codes you can put anywhere. New pieces get built the same way when stock changes. Nothing to scan, nothing to learn.</p><h2>Who builds the 3D content?</h2><p>With Matterport, the 3D twin comes from scans you make or pay for. The quality of the tour depends on the capture: phone scans work, a pro camera or a paid capture service looks better, and each one costs money or time. The views of your individual products are not part of it. A Matterport twin shows your shop, not a clickable version of each vase.</p><figure class="postimg"><img src="/blog/arqr360-vs-matterport-decor-stores/images/img1.jpg" alt="Customer browsing a decor showroom with vases, mirrors, and lamps on display" loading="lazy"><figcaption>A showroom tour brings your shop to the couch. It does not answer whether the piece fits the buyer\'s shelf.</figcaption></figure><p>With ARQR360, the view building is the service. We build the AR (Augmented Reality) views from your product photos and your real listed dimensions. You never touch modelling software and you never hire a 3D team. Your first view is free, so you can check the quality on your own phone before spending anything.</p><h2>True-to-size accuracy</h2><p>Matterport\'s twins are dimensionally accurate. Published comparisons put their measurements within about one percent of reality, which is why agents and architects trust them. Your customers can walk your shop and trust the distances they see.</p><p>ARQR360 views are built to your exact listed dimensions, so a 24-inch mirror appears 24 inches wide on the customer\'s wall. On accuracy both aim for the same thing. The difference is what the customer measures: your space in one case, your product in theirs.</p><figure class="postimg"><img src="/blog/arqr360-vs-matterport-decor-stores/images/img2.jpg" alt="Shopper pointing her phone at a bedroom wall with a mirror appearing at true scale" loading="lazy"><figcaption>The buyer checks your mirror against their own wall, at its real size.</figcaption></figure><h2>They answer different questions</h2><p>This is the part that matters. A Matterport tour answers: what does this shop look like, and what is on the shelves. That is a browsing question. It helps a customer who is deciding whether to visit or who wants the showroom experience online.</p><p>An ARQR360 view answers: will this exact piece fit my room. That is a buying question. Decor returns are almost always size returns. The vase looked right online and overwhelmed the side table. The mirror got swallowed by the wall. When the customer checks the real size before buying, those returns fall. For the returns side, read <a href="/blog/how-home-decor-stores-cut-returns-with-ar">how home decor stores cut returns with AR</a>.</p><p>Shopify reports that products with 3D or AR views convert about 94% better than products without. That is Shopify\'s own data, not ours. The mechanism is simple: shoppers who can see the true size hesitate less.</p><h2>When Matterport is the better pick</h2><p>Be honest about your store. Matterport fits better if your showroom itself sells: a beautiful space customers would visit if they could, a flagship you want visible worldwide, a trade buyer who wants to walk the floor before a big order. If the space is the product, scan the space.</p><h2>When ARQR360 is the better pick</h2><p>ARQR360 fits better if your sales happen on product pages, if your returns are size returns, and if you want each product to sell itself in the customer\'s room. If you would rather have the piece checked at true size on the buyer\'s wall than have the buyer tour your aisles, that is what ARQR360 does.</p><p>Some stores could use both. A Matterport tour of the showroom on the about page, and AR views on every product page where buying decisions happen. If you can only fund one, fund the one that answers the buying question.</p><p>See the buying question answered live. Open our <a href="/maison">decor demo</a> on your phone and check a piece against your own room. For the wider field, read <a href="/blog/best-ar-options-home-decor-stores">the best AR options for home decor stores</a>.</p><h2>The cheapest way to check if AR works for your store</h2><p>You do not have to commit to anything to find out. Send a photo of your bestselling vase or mirror and we will build you one true-to-size AR (Augmented Reality) view free. Put it on your own phone, check it against the real piece, and see if your customers would use it. If it does not convince you, you have spent nothing.</p><div class="faq"><h2>FAQ</h2><h3>Does Matterport put my products in the customer\'s room?</h3><p>No. Matterport captures your space, so customers walk through your showroom online. It does not create individual AR views of your products at true size in the customer\'s own room. Those are two different jobs.</p><h3>Do I need special hardware for Matterport?</h3><p>You can scan with a phone or a 360 camera. The best-looking captures use their pro LiDAR camera, which costs thousands of dollars, or their paid capture services. Budget the capture on top of the subscription.</p><h3>Can I try ARQR360 on my own product before paying?</h3><p>Yes. Send a photo of your bestselling vase or mirror and we will build you one true-to-size AR (Augmented Reality) view free, to try on your own phone. No commitment. Try the <a href="/maison">live decor demo</a> first: it opens in your phone browser, no install.</p></div>'})

# 2026-10-10 furniture non-comparison: UAE furniture stores cut returns
EXTRA_POSTS.append({'slug': 'how-uae-furniture-stores-cut-returns-with-ar', 'title': 'How UAE Furniture Stores Cut Returns with AR (No App Needed)', 'date': 'October 10, 2026', 'description': 'An honest look at why sofas come back to UAE furniture stores, and how true-to-size AR (Augmented Reality) views on product pages let shoppers check the fit before they buy. Written for independent retailers.', 'body': '<figure class="postimg"><img src="/blog/how-uae-furniture-stores-cut-returns-with-ar/images/hero.jpg" alt="A shopper in a Dubai apartment holding up her phone while a true-to-size sofa appears in AR" loading="lazy"><figcaption>A customer checks a sofa at true size in her own room, straight from the product page.</figcaption></figure><p>Picture a furniture store owner in Dubai. She sells sofas and dining tables from one showroom and a website that ships across the Emirates. A customer in Abu Dhabi buys a three-seater from her product page. It arrives, and it swallows the room. Back it comes. She pays the freight both ways and restocks a sofa she cannot resell as new. That story repeats a few times a month.</p><p>This is the plain-English version of why that happens and what actually fixes it.</p><h2>Why sofas come back</h2><p>Most furniture returns are not quality problems. They are size problems. The customer read the dimensions, looked at the photos, and guessed. Photos lie about scale. A 220cm sofa and a 180cm one look identical in a square product photo. The numbers are on the page, but numbers on a screen do not land in a room. People cannot feel 40cm of extra width from a spec table.</p><p>And the guess is almost always optimistic. A buyer furnishing a new apartment imagines the biggest piece fitting the biggest wall. The sofa arrives, the walkway behind it disappears, and the customer discovers what the floor plan never told them. The return request follows.</p><h2>The UAE part of the problem</h2><p>The UAE makes this worse in three specific ways. First, so much of the market is new. People move into empty apartments and furnish whole rooms from a phone, sight unseen. There is no old sofa to measure against. Second, from June to September almost nobody drives to a showroom unless they must. People shop from the couch in the air conditioning, and the product page has to do the job your showroom floor used to do. Third, a returned sofa in the Emirates is expensive to move. Bulky-item freight eats the margin twice, once there and once back, and a returned piece comes back dusty and tired.</p><p>Evening is when the browsing happens, after your shop is closed and your website is wide open. A shopper who can check the size at 10pm can buy at 10pm.</p><h2>What changes when the customer can check the fit first</h2><p>Now picture the same customer in Abu Dhabi. She taps a link on the product page. Her phone camera opens. She points it at her floor and the three-seater appears in her living room, at its true size. She walks around it. She sees whether it clears the balcony door and whether it eats the rug. She buys knowing, or she does not buy at all. Either way, the sofa does not come back over a surprise.</p><p>This is what true-to-size AR (Augmented Reality) does. It answers the one question photos cannot: will it fit my room. It opens in the phone browser from a link or a QR code. The customer installs nothing. Most people will not download an app to look at a sofa.</p><figure class="postimg"><img src="/blog/how-uae-furniture-stores-cut-returns-with-ar/images/img1.jpg" alt="A hand holding a phone showing an armchair at true scale against a living room wall" loading="lazy"><figcaption>The AR view answers the one question photos cannot: will it fit.</figcaption></figure><h2>What it actually takes</h2><p>You send photos of your bestselling pieces and the real listed dimensions. ARQR360 builds the true-to-size AR (Augmented Reality) views from them and hands you links and QR codes for each piece. About seven days from photos to a live catalogue. There is no platform to learn, no 3D team on your side, no developer work. We worked the full maths in our <a href="/blog/ar-cost-small-furniture-store-pricing">pricing breakdown for furniture stores</a>.</p><h2>Where the link lives</h2><p>Put the link next to the price on every product page, where the buying decision happens. A link buried in a menu nobody opens does nothing. In the shop, the QR code goes on the floor tags: a browsing customer scans the sofa they are standing in front of, checks it in their own room later that night, and comes back decided. That is the whole system.</p><figure class="postimg"><img src="/blog/how-uae-furniture-stores-cut-returns-with-ar/images/img2.jpg" alt="A shopper in a Dubai furniture showroom pointing her phone at the tag on a sofa" loading="lazy"><figcaption>Floor tags with QR codes let browsers check pieces at home and come back decided.</figcaption></figure><h2>The returns maths</h2><p>Be honest about it. One avoided sofa return pays for a catalogue like this many times over. ARQR360 publishes its price: $199 one-time setup and $19 a month, locked for life for the first 50 stores, AR (Augmented Reality) views included. No product cap, no per-view fee. Your first view is free, so you can check the quality on your own phone before spending anything.</p><p>One more number, and it is Shopify\'s own data, not ours: products with 3D or AR views convert about 94% better than products without. Whatever number your store gets, the mechanism is the same. Shoppers who can see the true size hesitate less and return less.</p><h2>The cheapest way to check if it works for your store</h2><p>You do not have to commit to anything to find out. Send a photo of your bestselling sofa or armchair and we will build you one true-to-size AR (Augmented Reality) view free. Put it on your own phone, check it against the real piece, and see if your customers would use it. If it does not convince you, you have spent nothing. Try the <a href="/novara">live furniture demo</a> first: it opens in your phone browser, no install.</p><div class="faq"><h2>FAQ</h2><h3>Do my customers need an app?</h3><p>No. Each piece gets a link and a QR code that opens the AR (Augmented Reality) view in the phone browser. Try the <a href="/novara">live furniture demo</a> on your own phone. If a provider needs their app installed, most customers will never use it.</p><h3>How long before my pieces are live?</h3><p>With ARQR360, about seven days from your photos arriving. Ask any provider for a written timeline for your catalogue size before you commit.</p><h3>What does it cost a small furniture store?</h3><p>ARQR360 is flat and public: $199 one-time setup and $19 a month, locked for life for the first 50 stores, AR (Augmented Reality) views included. The first test view is free.</p></div>'})
# 2026-10-10 footwear comparison: vs Cappasity (rotation rule: 1 of last 3 was a comparison)
EXTRA_POSTS.append({'slug': 'arqr360-vs-cappasity-footwear-stores', 'title': 'ARQR360 vs Cappasity: What a Footwear Store Actually Gets from Each', 'date': 'October 10, 2026', 'description': 'Comparing Cappasity and ARQR360 for your shoe store? An honest look at price, setup effort, true-to-size accuracy, and who builds the 3D models, written for independent footwear retailers.', 'body': '<figure class="postimg"><img src="/blog/arqr360-vs-cappasity-footwear-stores/images/hero.jpg" alt="A shoe store owner photographing a leather boot on a turntable to create 3D and AR content" loading="lazy"><figcaption>Digitizing a shoe starts with shooting it. The question is who does the shooting.</figcaption></figure><p>Picture a shoe store owner in Manchester. She sells leather boots and running shoes online and from one small shop. Her product pages get decent traffic. Shoppers zoom in on the photos, check the size guide, then close the tab. The shoes sit unsold.</p><p>She knows the fix is letting people see the shoe in 3D and try it in AR (Augmented Reality). So she starts comparing the tools, and one name keeps coming up: Cappasity. Then she finds ARQR360. This is the honest version of how they compare.</p><h2>What Cappasity is</h2><p>Cappasity is a 3D imaging platform for e-commerce. It has three parts: a SaaS platform, Easy 3D Scan software for making the content, and an API and SDK for embedding it into websites and apps. Their headline promise is speed: about 3 minutes per SKU to create a 3D view and embed it in your store.</p><p>The core idea is shoot-to-3D. You shoot a video of your product with a digital camera, run it through Easy 3D Scan, and the software produces a 3D image you upload to the platform and embed. If you already own 3D models of your shoes, you can upload those directly, switch on the AR view, and the platform generates the QR codes for you automatically.</p><p>This is a do-it-yourself platform in the true sense. You run the digitization. Cappasity supplies the software and the hosting.</p><h2>What ARQR360 is</h2><p>ARQR360 is a done-for-you AR catalogue for independent footwear retailers. You send photos of your bestselling shoes and their real listed dimensions, and we build true-to-size AR (Augmented Reality) views from them. You get links and QR codes that open the try-on in any phone browser. No app. No platform to learn. No shooting studio to build.</p><p>The audience is different. ARQR360 is built for stores that sell finished shoes off the shelf and want AR live this month, not brands building a digitization pipeline.</p><h2>Price: a platform subscription vs one public flat rate</h2><p>Cappasity offers a free Shopify app, but the free account is tight: 2 free embeds, 2,000 views a month, and a watermark on the views. A real catalogue needs a paid plan on top of that. Then there is the part nobody puts on a pricing page: the shooting setup.</p><p>ARQR360 publishes its price: $199 one-time setup and $19 a month, locked for life for the first 50 stores. The model building is included. There is no product cap, no per-view fee, and no studio equipment to buy.</p><h2>Setup effort: a shooting studio vs sending photos</h2><figure class="postimg"><img src="/blog/arqr360-vs-cappasity-footwear-stores/images/img2.jpg" alt="A sneaker on a motorized turntable under studio lights being filmed for 3D digitization" loading="lazy"><figcaption>The Cappasity route means a real shooting setup for every product you digitize.</figcaption></figure><p>On the Cappasity route, you shoot a video of about 1.2 spins per product. For high-quality results they recommend a 4K camera, studio lighting, and a motorized turntable. If you do not own that gear or the hours to learn it, they point you to their global network of product imaging experts. Then you process each video in Easy 3D Scan, upload the result, and embed it in your store. Once the setup exists, they say one SKU takes about 3 minutes.</p><p>That is fast per shoe, and it is the right shape for a retailer digitizing hundreds of SKUs. For a shop with 40 styles, the setup is the project: the gear, the learning, and the weekly shooting sessions. Every new season of stock goes through the same process.</p><p>With ARQR360 the project is: send photos, get a working AR catalogue back in about seven days. The links go on your product pages. The QR codes go in the shop. There is nothing to buy, learn, or maintain. New season stock follows the same process: send the photos.</p><h2>True-to-size accuracy</h2><figure class="postimg"><img src="/blog/arqr360-vs-cappasity-footwear-stores/images/img1.jpg" alt="A shopper pointing a phone at her foot to see a sneaker in augmented reality at true size" loading="lazy"><figcaption>Whatever route you take, the try-on has to match the real shoe. That is the part worth verifying.</figcaption></figure><p>Cappasity builds its views from video frames, and they market the result as an AR Hologram made in about 20 minutes with no manual 3D modelling. It looks impressive. The question to ask them in writing is how they verify the scale against the real shoe, because a try-on that is even slightly off teaches the customer that the tool cannot be trusted.</p><p>ARQR360 builds each AR (Augmented Reality) view from your product photos against the real listed dimensions, and you check it against the actual shoe on your own phone before it goes live. The honest difference is not the technology. It is what you have to go through to get your first ten shoes live, and what it costs to keep them there.</p><p>One more number, and it comes from Shopify, not from us: products with 3D or AR views convert about 94% better than products without. Whichever route you take, a true-to-size view beats a flat photo.</p><h2>Who each one fits</h2><p><strong>Cappasity makes sense if:</strong></p><ul><li>You are a brand or a large retailer planning to digitize hundreds of SKUs</li><li>You have, or want to build, an in-house shooting setup</li><li>You want API-level control over how the 3D content lives on your site</li><li>You have someone on the team who can own the digitization work week to week</li></ul><p><strong>ARQR360 is designed for stores like yours if:</strong></p><ul><li>You are an independent shoe retailer or a small chain</li><li>You sell finished shoes and hold no CAD files</li><li>You run Shopify, WooCommerce, or a simple website</li><li>You want shoppers trying shoes on in AR this month, not next quarter</li><li>You need one public price with no surprises</li></ul><h2>The question to ask both vendors</h2><p>Before you sign anything, ask both of us the same four questions in writing: what is the full annual cost including the shooting setup or the model building, who does the digitization work and what equipment does it need, what happens to your content if you end the contract, and does the customer need to download an app. The answers will tell you which tool fits your store faster than any feature list.</p><p>For the wider market, read <a href="/blog/best-ar-tools-footwear-stores-2026">the best AR tools for footwear stores in 2026</a> and <a href="/blog/arqr360-vs-seek-footwear-stores">our ARQR360 vs Seek comparison</a>.</p><h2>The cheapest way to check if AR works for your store</h2><p>You do not have to commit to anything to find out. Send a photo of your bestselling shoe and we will build you one true-to-size AR (Augmented Reality) view free. Put it on your own foot, check it against the real shoe, and see if your customers would use it. If it does not convince you, you have spent nothing. Try the <a href="/corso">live footwear demo</a> first: it opens in your phone browser, no install.</p><div class="faq"><h2>FAQ</h2><h3>How much does Cappasity cost?</h3><p>Their Shopify app is free to install, but the free account only covers 2 embeds and 2,000 views a month, with a watermark. A real catalogue needs a paid plan, plus the shooting setup: a 4K camera, studio lighting, and a turntable, or a hired imaging expert from their network. ARQR360 is flat and public: $199 one-time setup and $19 a month, with all model building included.</p><h3>Who does the digitization work?</h3><p>With Cappasity, you do: you shoot the videos, process them in Easy 3D Scan, and embed the results, or you hire from their imaging network. With ARQR360, we do: send your product photos and real listed dimensions, and we build the AR (Augmented Reality) views for you. The first test view is free, so you can check the quality before spending anything.</p><h3>Do my customers need to download an app?</h3><p>No, not with either option. Both open the AR view in the phone browser, and Cappasity generates the QR codes automatically. The difference is what it takes your store to get there: a shooting setup you run yourself, or a photo you send us. Try the <a href="/corso">live footwear demo</a> on your own phone and see.</p></div>'})
EXTRA_POSTS.append({"slug": "the-payback-maths-ar-small-decor-store","title": "The Payback Maths: Does AR Earn Its Keep at a Small Decor Store?","date": "October 11, 2026","description": "A plain-English payback check for small home decor stores: what AR actually costs, where the money comes back, and a five-line maths test you can run on your own numbers before spending a dollar.","body": "<figure class=\"postimg\"><img src=\"/blog/the-payback-maths-ar-small-decor-store/images/hero.jpg\" alt=\"A shopper in a bright living room checking a table lamp at true size on her phone screen\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>One phone, one lamp, true size. That is the whole product.</figcaption></figure><p>Picture a home decor store owner in Amsterdam. She sells lamps, mirrors, vases, and wall pieces from one shop and a website that ships across the Netherlands and Belgium. AR keeps coming up in her searches. Every article tells her it is the future. None of them answers the only question she actually has. Will it pay for itself at a store her size.</p><p>This is the plain-English answer, with the maths.</p><h2>What you actually pay</h2><p>There are two honest routes to AR for a small decor store.</p><p>Route one is self-serve. AR viewer apps on the app stores cost a small monthly fee, often under forty dollars, and put a 3D viewer on your product pages. But they are viewers only. You supply every model yourself, usually commissioned from 3D artists at roughly 100 to 500 dollars per product. A decor store with 120 pieces needs a model for each one before the subscription does anything. We worked the same maths in our <a href=\"/blog/ar-cost-small-decor-store-pricing\">decor pricing breakdown</a>. The software is cheap. The models are the project.</p><p>Route two is done for you. You send photos of your bestsellers and their real listed dimensions. ARQR360 builds the true-to-size AR (Augmented Reality) views from them, hosts them, and hands you links and QR codes for each piece. The price is flat and public: $199 one-time setup and $19 a month, locked for life for the first 50 stores, every view included. About seven days from photos to live views. Your first view is free, so you can check the quality on your own phone before spending anything.</p><h2>Where the money comes back</h2><p>AR pays a decor store back in two ways, and both are about size.</p><p>The first is returns. Most decor returns are size returns. A lamp that looked fine online arrives and looks lost on the sideboard. A mirror is smaller on the wall than it was in the photo. The customer is not angry at the product. They guessed the size and guessed wrong. Each of those returns costs you shipping both ways, plus the inspection and relisting work, plus a piece you can no longer sell as new. Cut the guessing and you cut the cheapest losses in the business.</p><figure class=\"postimg\"><img src=\"/blog/the-payback-maths-ar-small-decor-store/images/img2.jpg\" alt=\"A hand taping up a shipping box with a lamp packed inside for return\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Every size return costs shipping both ways and a relisting. Guessing is expensive.</figcaption></figure><p>The second is conversion. Shoppers hesitate on decor for one reason: they cannot see the scale. Numbers on a spec tab do not translate into a room. When the buyer can point a phone at their wall and see the mirror at its true size, the hesitation shrinks. Shopify reports that products with 3D or AR views convert about 94% better than products without. That is Shopify's own data, not ours, and your number will be your own. But the mechanism is plain. Shoppers who can see the true size buy more confidently and send less back.</p><h2>The five-line payback check</h2><p>Do the maths on paper before you spend anything. These are round numbers, not a promise. Fill in your own.</p><ul><li><strong>Your online orders a month.</strong> Say 300.</li><li><strong>Your return rate.</strong> Say 1 in 20 orders comes back, 15 returns a month.</li><li><strong>Your cost per return.</strong> Shipping both ways plus the handling. Say 12 euros.</li><li><strong>The monthly leak.</strong> 15 returns times 12 euros is 180 euros a month, gone.</li><li><strong>The AR cost.</strong> $19 a month after a $199 setup, views included.</li></ul><p>If AR stops even a quarter of those size returns, the leak drops by 45 euros a month, and the service costs a fraction of that. Add one extra sale a week from shoppers who bought because they could see the true size, and the case is closed. This is a made-up store with round numbers, but the structure is real. Your own five lines will tell you the truth faster than any sales page.</p><figure class=\"postimg\"><img src=\"/blog/the-payback-maths-ar-small-decor-store/images/img1.jpg\" alt=\"A shopper checking whether a mirror fits his wall by pointing his phone at it\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>A shopper checks the size on his own wall before ordering. Returns never start.</figcaption></figure><h2>The mistakes that make AR lose money</h2><p>AR does not pay for itself on its own. The stores where it flops make the same four mistakes.</p><ul><li><strong>The buried link.</strong> The AR view lives in a gallery page nobody opens. It belongs next to the buy button, where the size doubt lives.</li><li><strong>The app.</strong> If the customer must install an app, most of them will not. Browser AR opens from a link in seconds. That is the version that actually gets used.</li><li><strong>The whole catalogue on day one.</strong> You do not need 200 views to learn. Twenty bestsellers teach you what customers actually do with it.</li><li><strong>Paying per model forever.</strong> Per-model fees quietly dwarf subscriptions on catalogues that change. Know the full annual cost before you sign.</li></ul><h2>The cheapest way to check if it works for your store</h2><p>You do not have to believe any article, including this one. Send a photo of your bestselling lamp or mirror and we will build you one true-to-size AR (Augmented Reality) view free. Put it on your own phone, check it against the real piece, and run your own five lines with your own numbers. If it does not convince you, you have spent nothing. Try the <a href=\"/maison\">live decor demo</a> first: it opens in your phone browser, no install.</p><div class=\"faq\"><h2>FAQ</h2><h3>Does AR cut returns or just sell more?</h3><p>Both, but only size-related returns. A lamp that looked too small still comes back if the fault was quality. AR fixes the guessing problem, which is the biggest slice of decor returns. Run your own return reasons and see how many were about size.</p><h3>My store is mostly offline. Does AR still pay?</h3><p>That is where the QR code earns its keep. A tag next to the floor lamp lets a browsing customer check the piece in their own room that evening and come back decided. Try the <a href=\"/maison\">live decor demo</a> on your phone and picture it on your own shop floor.</p><h3>How do I know it works before I pay?</h3><p>Do not pay. Send a photo of your bestseller and we build one true-to-size AR (Augmented Reality) view free. Check it against the real piece on your own phone. That is the whole test.</p></div>"})

